const PASSAGE_A = `For much of the history of Western philosophy, the dominant picture of how language works was deceptively simple: words mean things by standing in for them. On the referentialist view, most thoroughly developed in Russell's theory of descriptions and the early Wittgenstein's picture theory of meaning, language is essentially a representational medium. A sentence is true when it accurately pictures a state of affairs in the world; a name is meaningful when it is attached to an object. This picture has a certain intuitive appeal. When a child first learns language, she does seem to learn by ostension — by being shown an object while hearing the word. The word "cat" seems to mean the animal, not some further abstraction.

The later Wittgenstein repudiated this picture with unusual force — unusual because he was repudiating his own earlier work. In the Philosophical Investigations, Wittgenstein argued that the referentialist account misconceives the relationship between words and the world. The meaning of a word, he insisted, is not the object it refers to but the way it is used — its role in the form of life that surrounds it. This becomes clear when we consider words that have no obvious referent. What does "game" refer to? There is no single property shared by all and only games; rather, games share a complicated network of overlapping similarities, what Wittgenstein called "family resemblances." The attempt to find a definition — an essence — is not just difficult but misguided.

The concept of "language games" captures something further: that words acquire meaning only within practices, and practices are embedded in social forms of life. The word "pain," for instance, does not mean a private inner sensation that each person accesses independently; it is a move within a social practice of pain-attribution that is governed by public criteria. If the word "pain" meant only a private mental object, we could never know whether we were using it the same way as anyone else — and the appearance of shared meaning would be illusory. This is sometimes called the Private Language Argument, and it represents Wittgenstein's most direct attack on the picture that meaning is a relationship between minds and objects.

Critics of Wittgenstein's later philosophy point out that the use theory faces its own difficulties. If meaning is use, then changing use changes meaning — but then it becomes obscure how language could be used to challenge or reform social practices. If the criteria for applying "justice" are given by how people actually use the term, then the radical who claims that prevailing social arrangements are unjust is simply using the word incorrectly. The use theory, the objection runs, threatens to reduce meaning to convention and thereby insulate current practices from critique.

Defenders of Wittgenstein respond that this objection caricatures the view. Use-theory does not imply that current use is fixed and unchallengeable; language itself contains tools for internal critique, and forms of life are not static. The more important point is that meaning cannot float free of all practice. Language that is untethered from any shared form of life — that makes no contact with observable behavior — does not acquire deeper private meaning; it ceases to mean anything at all.`

const PASSAGE_B = `Thomas Kuhn's The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, published in 1962, fundamentally altered how historians and philosophers understand the development of science. Before Kuhn, the dominant picture was one of steady accumulation: science grows by adding new truths to a storehouse of established knowledge. Failed theories are discarded; confirmed ones are retained; the edifice grows. This picture had a reassuring linearity — even if progress was sometimes slow, the direction was always forward.

Kuhn challenged this picture by distinguishing between two quite different modes of scientific activity. "Normal science" is the workaday activity of puzzle-solving within an established framework — what Kuhn called a "paradigm." A paradigm is not merely a theory; it is an entire framework of assumptions, exemplary problems, instruments, and standards of good science. Scientists working within a paradigm do not typically question it; they take its core commitments for granted and work to extend its reach. Normal science is, in this sense, deeply conservative: its success depends on a willingness to treat the paradigm as authoritative.

But paradigms generate anomalies — phenomena that the existing framework cannot easily accommodate. In normal science, anomalies are set aside as puzzles to be solved later. If anomalies accumulate and resist resolution, however, they produce a crisis: a growing sense that something fundamental is wrong with the framework. Crisis opens the door to revolution — the replacement of one paradigm by another. Copernicus replaced Ptolemy; Lavoisier replaced phlogiston theory; Einstein replaced Newton. These were not additions to existing science; they were replacements of one framework by a fundamentally different one.

What makes revolutions genuinely revolutionary, for Kuhn, is what he called "incommensurability." Scientists working within different paradigms do not simply disagree about facts; they inhabit different worlds, asking different questions and using key terms with different meanings. When a Newtonian physicist and a relativist use the word "mass," they are not necessarily talking about the same thing — the term has different implications within each theoretical framework. This means there is no neutral language in which the two paradigms can be compared point by point, and no algorithm for deciding which framework is better.

Critics charged that Kuhn's account made scientific revolutions seem irrational — as if scientists chose paradigms the way they chose political affiliations or religious beliefs. Kuhn resisted this reading. He maintained that there were good reasons, even if not algorithmic ones, for preferring one paradigm over another: predictive accuracy, fruitfulness, simplicity, breadth of application. But these reasons do not constitute a decision procedure; different scientists can weigh them differently and still be rational. Scientific communities ultimately change paradigms, and the change is not arbitrary — but neither is it purely logical.`

const PASSAGE_C = `The central problem of political legitimacy — why should citizens be obligated to obey laws they did not personally choose? — has received a range of answers in the liberal tradition. The most familiar is the consent theory: laws are legitimate when they derive, directly or indirectly, from the consent of those who will be governed by them. This answer has always faced the obvious difficulty that in large modern states, meaningful individual consent is at best metaphorical — citizens do not sign social contracts, and the existence of "tacit consent" has been a source of philosophical embarrassment since Hume first pointed out that for most people the alternative to consent is emigration, which is hardly a free choice.

A more sophisticated liberal response to the legitimacy question is the deliberative democratic account, associated above all with Jürgen Habermas. On this view, political decisions are legitimate not because all citizens consented to them but because they are the outcome of a process of rational public deliberation in which all affected parties have the opportunity to participate and advance reasons for their positions. The legitimating force derives not from the act of consent but from the quality of the deliberative process: if the process is inclusive, reason-governed, and free from domination and strategic manipulation, the outcomes have a claim to authority even for those who voted against them.

The deliberative account has an important implication for the content of permissible political argument. Habermas and John Rawls both argued, in different ways, that legitimate political argument must restrict itself to reasons that can in principle be accepted by all citizens — what Rawls called "public reason." Appealing to the authority of a particular religious tradition, for instance, is not legitimate public reason because it depends on premises that citizens who do not share that tradition cannot be expected to accept. Only arguments from premises that all reasonable citizens can recognize as valid, regardless of their comprehensive philosophical or religious commitments, belong to the domain of public reason.

Critics of the public reason requirement have questioned whether the distinction between public and non-public reasons can be sustained. First, the line between a "comprehensive doctrine" and a genuinely public reason is rarely clear in practice: many of the intuitions that underlie liberal democratic politics have roots in particular historical and religious traditions and cannot be fully severed from them. Second, the requirement of public reason may systematically disadvantage communities whose political language is inseparable from their religious or cultural identity — communities for whom the demand to translate their convictions into neutral liberal terms is effectively a demand to silence themselves.

A third difficulty concerns the epistemic ambitions of deliberative theory. If legitimate outcomes require genuinely rational deliberation, then political systems that produce outcomes through strategic bargaining, bloc voting, or interest-group pressure cannot generate legitimacy — a verdict that seems to exclude most actual democratic politics. The deliberative theorist can respond that actual politics is judged by an ideal that it only imperfectly realizes, and that the ideal can function as a critical standard even if never fully instantiated. But this response raises the question of how much imperfection is compatible with genuine legitimacy, and on this the theory tends to be silent.`

const PASSAGE_D = `The rise of the novel as a literary form in eighteenth-century Europe was not merely a commercial phenomenon or an accident of printing technology. It was, argues a tradition of literary sociology extending from Georg Lukács through Ian Watt, the expression of a new social world — the world of bourgeois individualism. The novel's defining formal innovation was the representation of interiority: for the first time in literary history, readers were given systematic access to the inner life of ordinary individuals — their thoughts, desires, hesitations, and self-deceiving rationalizations. This formal choice was not politically neutral. To make the consciousness of an ordinary person the center of a narrative is already to assert that ordinary persons have a consciousness worth centering — a claim that earlier aristocratic literary forms implicitly denied.

The novel thus encoded and promoted the values of the rising middle class: the importance of individual experience, the moral significance of private feeling, the idea that a person's worth is determined not by birth but by character. But literary sociologists have noted a complication: the novel did not merely reflect these values; it helped construct them. Reading novels trained readers in practices of interiority and empathy that were themselves relatively novel. The spread of novel-reading in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries can be connected to the spread of certain emotional habits — what historians have called the "civilizing process" — that were partly constituted by the reading practice itself.

This raises a question about the political valence of the form. On one reading, the novel is an inherently progressive genre: by making the inner lives of servants, women, and social outsiders narratable, it democratized sympathy and extended the moral community. Fielding's Tom Jones and Richardson's Pamela — whatever their limitations — brought to literary representation classes of people who had previously been invisible. On an alternative reading, however, the novel is a fundamentally conservative genre: its ideology of individualism naturalizes a social order in which collective action and structural analysis are replaced by the moral dramas of private persons. The system of exploitation that produces the servant's condition is less interesting to the novel than the servant's feelings about it.

This tension is not merely a historical curiosity. Contemporary debates about the political function of literary fiction reproduce the same dialectic. Critics who defend literature's capacity for moral education typically point to the novel's power to cultivate empathy — to make us imaginatively inhabit experiences not our own. Critics who are skeptical of this power point out that the empathy produced by novel-reading is typically directed at individual characters in individual situations, and does not obviously transfer to political solidarity with structural causes. Reading a harrowing novel about poverty may produce sympathy for a fictional character without producing any disposition to address the conditions that generate poverty.

The argument has never been conclusively resolved — partly because its empirical component has proven methodologically intractable. But what both sides of the debate tend to agree on is that the novel is not a neutral form: its formal features — point of view, interiority, narrative arc, the centering of individual experience — are not innocent of politics. The question is which politics, and whether they are fixed or historically variable.`

const PASSAGE_E = `The commonsense picture of memory treats it as a faculty of the individual mind: I remember my grandmother's house, my first day of school, the smell of a particular autumn. Memory, on this picture, is personal and private — a subjective archive of personal experience. Maurice Halbwachs, the French sociologist whose work on collective memory has been enormously influential, challenged this picture at its foundations. For Halbwachs, memory is not primarily an individual psychological process; it is an essentially social one. We remember through and with social groups; our memories are sustained, shaped, and often generated by the social frameworks within which we live.

The evidence for this claim lies in the peculiar way our memories depend on social context. When we lose contact with a social group — through death, migration, or social rupture — we often find that our memories of the period associated with that group begin to fade or transform. Conversely, shared commemorative practices — rituals, memorials, anniversaries — actively produce memories and keep them alive in forms that serve present social functions. The memory of a nation's founding, for instance, is not a simple retrieval of a past event; it is an ongoing social construction that is revised with each generation according to present needs and conflicts.

This constructive character of collective memory raises uncomfortable political questions. If memories are not merely retrieved but constructed, then those who control commemorative practices — states, churches, dominant social groups — have the power to shape what communities remember and therefore who they are. The history of politically imposed amnesia — revolutionary regimes that suppressed the memory of their predecessors, colonial powers that overwrote indigenous histories — is one illustration of this power. But the more subtle and pervasive phenomenon is the way that dominant memory frameworks marginalize the memories of subordinate groups, not through explicit suppression but through the simple dominance of certain narratives in public culture.

Jan Assmann, extending Halbwachs's framework, distinguished between "communicative memory" — the living memory of events within living experience, typically extending back two to three generations — and "cultural memory" — the formalized, institutionalized memory of a more distant past, preserved through texts, rituals, and monuments. Cultural memory differs from communicative memory not merely in its temporal scope but in its social function: where communicative memory is fluid, personal, and subject to negotiation, cultural memory is fixed, authoritative, and identity-constituting. What a society takes itself to remember about its ancient past — its founding myths, sacred histories, canonical texts — functions as the bedrock of collective identity.

Critics of the Halbwachs-Assmann tradition argue that the emphasis on social construction risks collapsing the distinction between memory and history, and between authentic remembering and ideological fabrication. If memories are always socially constructed, the critic asks, what distinguishes the genuine memory of a traumatic event from a politically motivated myth dressed in the language of remembrance? Defenders of the tradition respond that the social construction of memory does not make it false or arbitrary; social construction is compatible with genuine cognitive contact with the past. The question is not whether memory is socially shaped — it always is — but whether the shaping serves or distorts understanding.`

